# HOTFOX — ROADMAP PROGRESSION RULE

This rule is ALWAYS ACTIVE.

Canonical roadmap:

`docs/HOTFOX_ROADMAP.md`

Current phase:

`2.5 Privacy Controls / Smart Routing`

## 1. Read the roadmap before phase work

Before planning, implementing or closing a roadmap phase, read the corresponding section in `docs/HOTFOX_ROADMAP.md`.

Do not rely on an older chat summary when the repository roadmap is available.

## 2. Preserve historical work

Do not delete, rewrite or invalidate completed 2.2/2.3 work simply because later phases exist.

Preserve earlier code paths and reviewer evidence unless the current phase uncovers a real regression or architectural defect.

## 3. Work sequentially

Canonical sequence:

`2.5 Privacy Controls / Smart Routing`
→ `2.6 HotFox Shadow / Stealth & Resilience`
→ `2.7 Operations / Release Infrastructure`
→ `2.8 HotFox Autopilot / Adaptive Protection`
→ `2.9 Premium Android Experience`
→ `3.0 Mature HotFox Platform`

Do not skip directly to a later phase.

Do not blend several future phases into one uncontrolled implementation batch.

Small prerequisite abstractions are allowed when they are genuinely necessary for the current phase.

## 4. Cost-bounded review policy

OpenAI checkpoint review is intentionally NOT run on every commit, every push or every CI run.

Ordinary implementation, refactor, test-fix and build-fix commits MUST NOT contain either review marker:

- `[hotfox-review]`
- `[hotfox-phase-exit]`

`[hotfox-review]` is reserved for an explicitly requested exceptional/manual checkpoint.

The canonical automatic engineering-exit marker is:

`[hotfox-phase-exit]`

Use it only on the final candidate commit for the current roadmap phase, after the phase implementation is believed complete and applicable local/engineering checks are ready for the full repository CI.

Do not create a separate no-code `[hotfox-review]` commit after CI merely to wake the reviewer. That causes redundant CI/build work and defeats the token/cost-saving design.

## 5. Automatic phase-exit review flow

Canonical phase closure is:

1. finish the required implementation;
2. run relevant local/unit/integration/static/lint checks where available;
3. fix real failures rather than disabling meaningful checks;
4. make the final phase candidate commit include `[hotfox-phase-exit]` exactly once;
5. push it and allow the normal full HotFox CI to run;
6. if CI is red, fix the failure with ordinary commits and do NOT invoke OpenAI review;
7. when a `[hotfox-phase-exit]` candidate receives green full CI, the GitHub phase-exit orchestrator automatically dispatches exactly one AI checkpoint review for that PR head;
8. do not manually duplicate that review while it is queued/running;
9. if the reviewer reports P0/P1, fix every substantiated blocker using ordinary commits;
10. once those fixes and applicable checks are ready, make the next final candidate include `[hotfox-phase-exit]` and let green CI trigger the next review automatically;
11. if the reviewer reports no substantiated P0/P1, record `<phase> ENGINEERING COMPLETE — physical release validation deferred.`;
12. update trusted current-phase context to the next roadmap phase;
13. immediately start the next roadmap phase.

P2-only findings are non-blocking unless explicitly promoted by the owner.

The GitHub automation is designed so expensive reviewer tokens are spent only on green engineering-exit candidates, not on ordinary development churn or known-red builds.

## 6. Automatic continuation after APPROVED

For an automatic `phase_exit` review, an APPROVED result posts a single `@cursor` continuation handoff.

Treat that handoff as authorization to:

- mark the current phase `ENGINEERING COMPLETE — physical release validation deferred`;
- update the repository's trusted current-phase context;
- continue immediately with the next roadmap phase.

Do not wait for a separate user confirmation between roadmap engineering phases.

Do not create multiple Cursor agents for the same APPROVED handoff when one is already active.

## 7. Do not stop for physical Android testing between phases

Physical Android testing is not a transition requirement for 2.4 -> 2.5 -> 2.6 -> 2.7 -> 2.8 -> 2.9 -> 3.0.

Do not ask the owner for a physical device test as a prerequisite for continuing this sequence.

Do not wait idle for such a test.

Do not classify the absence of deferred physical testing as a phase P0/P1.

Never fabricate physical test evidence.

## 8. Final physical release gate only

After `3.0 ENGINEERING COMPLETE`, stop automatic engineering phase progression because the canonical engineering roadmap is complete.

At that point the project enters:

`FINAL RELEASE DEVICE GATE`

The real-device acceptance suite in `docs/HOTFOX_ROADMAP.md` must actually pass before claiming:

`RELEASE READY`

The owner may explicitly cut a release candidate earlier and invoke the final release device gate for that release, but Cursor must not assume such a cut on its own.

## 9. Reviewer scope discipline

During each phase, review:

- regressions of completed guarantees;
- concrete defects in the currently implemented phase;
- build/test/security issues evidenced by current code.

Do not report unimplemented future roadmap functionality as a current P0/P1.

## 10. Truthful VPN and commerce guarantees always win

Roadmap progression must never weaken:

- real VpnService/TUN -> HEV/tun2socks -> Xray traffic forwarding;
- canonical protected-state truth;
- DNS and IPv6 leak policy;
- bounded lifecycle/reconnect behavior;
- backend-authoritative payment and entitlement;
- secret handling;
- manual subscription compatibility where still in scope;
- AUTO/manual selection intent.

Do not trade correctness for faster milestone completion.

## 11. Communication

All phase reports, `[hotfox-review]` summaries, automatic phase-exit summaries, P0/P1/P2 explanations and transition notices must follow the HotFox Russian communication rule.

Canonical technical identifiers may remain in English.
